  new(74,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U05:L07","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-5/lesson-7/preparation.html","fc5517a9c6a940a221cfae873c6739ac981bb60d8f1157c14068adeea61cf2ee",
  ["CCSS:2.MD.B.6","CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.B.2"],
  ["Learning goal: You will work with place value and practice addition and subtraction within 100.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 2 of the Mystery Number center, which was first introduced in grade 1. In this new stage, called Three-digit Numbers, you use clues based on place value to identify the number being described.",
   "Key ideas:\n- Practice addition and subtraction within 100\n- Use place value to describe and identify a number\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.",
   "Example 1: Decide if each statement is true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. \\(100 > 99\\) \\(100 < 99 + 1\\) \\(98 + 3 > 100\\) \\(50 + 50 + 50 > 100\\)\nApproach: “How did you know that \\(100 < 99 + 1\\) was false?” (99 plus 1 more is 100. When we count, 100 is the next number. You are equal.)",
   "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
   "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
   "You will work with place value and practice addition and subtraction within 100. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."],
  ["IM2021 original Grade2 Unit5 Lesson7 Center Day 1 (optional). Original True/False statements:100>99 TRUE;100<99+1 FALSE;98+3>100 TRUE;50+50+50>100 TRUE. OPTIONAL Activity1 Mystery Number Stage2: REAL partner draws3 number cards from0–9 (remove card10), forms 3-digit numeral, gives actual place-value clues and partner guesses. Activity2 choose Jump the Line Stage1, Mystery Number Stage2 or Number Puzzles Stages2–4, in real class center, NOT fictitious card outcomes. Source math: One hundred is exactly the next whole number after99. A three-digit number hto uses h hundreds, t tens, o ones; real digit cards may produce multiple valid clues or numbers. The original stage is optional classroom practice, not new required academic standard. Real teacher tasks: Actual original Mystery Number cards/clues, center selection, Jump the Line board, number puzzles, partner work and discussion educator observed; restricted cool-down source not copied.",
   "Lesson-specific rules: One hundred is exactly the next whole number after99. A three-digit number hto uses h hundreds, t tens, o ones; real digit cards may produce multiple valid clues or numbers. The original stage is optional classroom practice, not new required academic standard. Original source activity essential evidence: OPTIONAL Activity1 Mystery Number Stage2: REAL partner draws3 number cards from0–9 (remove card10), forms 3-digit numeral, gives actual place-value clues and partner guesses. Activity2 choose Jump the Line Stage1, Mystery Number Stage2 or Number Puzzles Stages2–4, in real class center, NOT fictitious card outcomes. Official standards: CCSS:2.MD.B.6, CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.OA.B.2.",
   "ORIGINAL SOURCE (teacher/public math tasks, paraphrased): 100>99,100<100 is false,98+3=101>100,150>100. Original center cards are physical and student outcomes unknown. The source-specific image, physical work and student response require actual educator inspection. Other generated examples in Edulytics are FIRST-PARTY, not copied publisher restricted assessment.",
   "1. Review true original publisher warm-up: Original True/False statements:100>99 TRUE;100<99+1 FALSE;98+3>100 TRUE;50+50+50>100 TRUE. 2. Examine source student-facing tasks and materials: OPTIONAL Activity1 Mystery Number Stage2: REAL partner draws3 number cards from0–9 (remove card10), forms 3-digit numeral, gives actual place-value clues and partner guesses. Activity2 choose Jump the Line Stage1, Mystery Number Stage2 or Number Puzzles Stages2–4, in real class center, NOT fictitious card outcomes. 3. Show original numeric and place-value strategies: One hundred is exactly the next whole number after99. A three-digit number hto uses h hundreds, t tens, o ones; real digit cards may produce multiple valid clues or numbers. The original stage is optional classroom practice, not new required academic standard. 4. Discuss plausible misconception and accurate solution with specific source values: 99+1 is EQUAL100, not more. Do not assert invented partner guesses were actual source results. A card '10' is removed from deck before a three-digit numeral game. 5. Finish actual source images/cards/games/drawings with educator; do NOT invent values not available as text. 6. Complete separate original Edulytics numeric practice with exact re-verification and teacher-graded open parts. 7. Preserve teacher assessment boundary: Actual original Mystery Number cards/clues, center selection, Jump the Line board, number puzzles, partner work and discussion educator observed; restricted cool-down source not copied.",
   "Mathematically inappropriate actions: 99+1 is EQUAL100, not more. Do not assert invented partner guesses were actual source results. A card '10' is removed from deck before a three-digit numeral game. Do not claim numeric answer certifies original image, physical block/card game, number-line dot or class oral practice.",
   "Source IM2021 G2 Unit5 L7. One hundred is exactly the next whole number after99. A three-digit number hto uses h hundreds, t tens, o ones; real digit cards may produce multiple valid clues or numbers. The original stage is optional classroom practice, not new required academic standard. Actual original Mystery Number cards/clues, center selection, Jump the Line board, number puzzles, partner work and discussion educator observed; restricted cool-down source not copied."]),
  new(75,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U05:L08","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-5/lesson-8/preparation.html","fad8307ed9d95054a77670152311bf80864c590cd8f1db54786f1d1a52d2505f",
  ["CCSS:2.MD.B.6","CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2"],
  ["Learning goal: You will locate and label three-digit numbers on number lines.\nIn previous lessons, you learned different ways to represent three-digit numbers. You also located numbers within 100 on a number line. In this lesson, you revisit the structure of the number line and use what you know about place value and counting by 10 and 100 to locate three-digit numbers on a number line (MP7).",
   "Key ideas:\n- Represent whole numbers up to 1,000 as lengths from 0 on a number line\n- Use skip-counting by tens and hundreds to locate whole numbers up to 1,000 on a number line\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.",
   "Example 1: What do you notice? What do you wonder? Locate and label 30, 300, and 3 on a number line. Label each point with a number it represents.\nApproach: “What patterns do you see?” (All 3 number lines show 10 jumps. You all start at zero, but end with different numbers.)\nExample 2: Locate and label each number on the number line. Label the tick marks with the numbers they represent if it helps. 700 472 940 356 590\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.",
   "Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.",
   "Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.",
   "You will locate and label three-digit numbers on number lines. In previous lessons, you learned different ways to represent three-digit numbers."],
  ["IM2021 original Grade2 Unit5 Lesson8 Three-digit Numbers on the Number Line. Original Choral Count by10 from0 to100 and by100 from0 to1000. This is the first Grade2 number-line encounter with1000; it is a scale endpoint, not a requirement to master units of thousand. Activity1 original three eleven-tick lines all anchored0, intervals scaled1,10 or100 respectively; locate3,30,300 on appropriate respective scales. Activity1 source 620–630 requires ONE-unit gaps, not hundreds. Activity2 genuine source plots numbers700,472,940,356,590, pupil labels original figure at correct tick positions using landmarks and unit scale. Source number-line graphics educator reviews. Source math: A number line may have one-, ten- or hundred-sized intervals but spacing WITHIN one given line must be uniform. Eleven ticks create ten intervals. From0 to1000 ten equal gaps each worth100. The coordinate472 on0..1000 lies between400 and500, not at a printed400 mark. Real teacher tasks: Original number line graphics, drawn locations, actual 620–630 scale and partner MLR8 discussion teacher supervised; no protected Cool-down.",
   "Lesson-specific rules: A number line may have one-, ten- or hundred-sized intervals but spacing WITHIN one given line must be uniform. Eleven ticks create ten intervals. From0 to1000 ten equal gaps each worth100. The coordinate472 on0..1000 lies between400 and500, not at a printed400 mark. Original source activity essential evidence: Activity1 original three eleven-tick lines all anchored0, intervals scaled1,10 or100 respectively; locate3,30,300 on appropriate respective scales. Activity1 source 620–630 requires ONE-unit gaps, not hundreds. Activity2 genuine source plots numbers700,472,940,356,590, pupil labels original figure at correct tick positions using landmarks and unit scale. Source number-line graphics educator reviews. Official standards: CCSS:2.MD.B.6, CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2.",
   "ORIGINAL SOURCE (teacher/public math tasks, paraphrased): 3 on0..10 by1,30 on0..100 by10,300 on0..1000 by100; source700,472,940,356,590 on actual source diagrams. The source-specific image, physical work and student response require actual educator inspection. Other generated examples in Edulytics are FIRST-PARTY, not copied publisher restricted assessment.",
   "1. Review true original publisher warm-up: Original Choral Count by10 from0 to100 and by100 from0 to1000. This is the first Grade2 number-line encounter with1000; it is a scale endpoint, not a requirement to master units of thousand. 2. Examine source student-facing tasks and materials: Activity1 original three eleven-tick lines all anchored0, intervals scaled1,10 or100 respectively; locate3,30,300 on appropriate respective scales. Activity1 source 620–630 requires ONE-unit gaps, not hundreds. Activity2 genuine source plots numbers700,472,940,356,590, pupil labels original figure at correct tick positions using landmarks and unit scale. Source number-line graphics educator reviews. 3. Show original numeric and place-value strategies: A number line may have one-, ten- or hundred-sized intervals but spacing WITHIN one given line must be uniform. Eleven ticks create ten intervals. From0 to1000 ten equal gaps each worth100. The coordinate472 on0..1000 lies between400 and500, not at a printed400 mark. 4. Discuss plausible misconception and accurate solution with specific source values: Treating equal physical spacing on differently scaled lines as equal numerical units. Counting eleven ticks as eleven gaps. Making a source graphic's actual dot location claim from just numeric answer rather than original image. 5. Finish actual source images/cards/games/drawings with educator; do NOT invent values not available as text. 6. Complete separate original Edulytics numeric practice with exact re-verification and teacher-graded open parts. 7. Preserve teacher assessment boundary: Original number line graphics, drawn locations, actual 620–630 scale and partner MLR8 discussion teacher supervised; no protected Cool-down.",
   "Mathematically inappropriate actions: Treating equal physical spacing on differently scaled lines as equal numerical units. Counting eleven ticks as eleven gaps. Making a source graphic's actual dot location claim from just numeric answer rather than original image. Do not claim numeric answer certifies original image, physical block/card game, number-line dot or class oral practice.",
   "Source IM2021 G2 Unit5 L8. A number line may have one-, ten- or hundred-sized intervals but spacing WITHIN one given line must be uniform. Eleven ticks create ten intervals. From0 to1000 ten equal gaps each worth100. The coordinate472 on0..1000 lies between400 and500, not at a printed400 mark. Original number line graphics, drawn locations, actual 620–630 scale and partner MLR8 discussion teacher supervised; no protected Cool-down."]),
  new(76,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U05:L09","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-5/lesson-9/preparation.html","e69fffdc4e17ba4c3984af198e764e462d5dabf84f25b6b84a694322e440a314",
  ["CCSS:2.MD.B.6","CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.4"],
  ["Learning goal: You will compare three-digit numbers using a number line.\nIn previous lessons, you estimated the location of numbers on the number line within 100 and learned to locate numbers within 1,000 on the number line. In this lesson, you use the relative position of numbers on the number line to compare three-digit numbers.",
   "Key ideas:\n- Compare three-digit numbers using the relative position of numbers on a number line\n- In previous lessons, you estimated the location of numbers on the number line within 100 and learned to locate numbers within 1,000 on the number line\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.",
   "Example 1: What number could this be? Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm}...\nApproach: Represent both quantities, compare their values, and state the relationship using words, symbols, or a clear explanation.\nExample 2: Each student compared 371 and 317, but represented their thinking in different ways. Diego I see 3 hundreds for each number. 317 only has 1 ten, but 371 has 7 tens. \\(371 > 317\\) Clare Each has 3 hundreds. 371 has 7 tens, but 317 only has 1 ten. \\(317 < 371\\) Jada Description: Number line. Scale 300 to 400 by tens. 11 evenly spaced tick marks. First tick...\nApproach: “Clare, Diego, and Jada represented your thinking in different ways. Whose method made it easier to see that 371 is greater than 317? Explain.” (Jada’s because you just have to look at which number is farther to the right. You can see 371 is farther from 0. Diego’s because I use diagrams and I can see quickly that the hundreds are the same and there are more tens, but some things were the same.) As needed, “Jada...",
   "Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.",
   "Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.",
   "You will compare three-digit numbers using a number line. In previous lessons, you estimated the location of numbers on the number line within 100 and learned to locate numbers within 1,000 on the number line."],
