   "Lesson-specific rules: Compare three-digit numbers by hundreds first, then tens, then ones. Equal hundreds371 vs317 differ in tens (7>1), so371>317. On equally spaced0-anchored number line a larger number lies farther right, even if visible diagram is cropped between300 and400. Original unknown estimation remains OPEN; no fabricated exact dot. Original source activity essential evidence: Activity1 original compare371 with317 by Diego base-ten picture, Clare digit reasoning, Jada authentic300..400 line with actual dots317 and371. Compare own true dots483 and443 on original400..500 number line;483>443. Activity2 multiple comparisons in original lesson using number lines; genuine student drawings need educator. Official standards: CCSS:2.MD.B.6, CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.A.4.",
   "ORIGINAL SOURCE (teacher/public math tasks, paraphrased): Source371>317;317<371; source483>443; warm source open point strictly between300 and400, no unique answer. The source-specific image, physical work and student response require actual educator inspection. Other generated examples in Edulytics are FIRST-PARTY, not copied publisher restricted assessment.",
   "1. Review true original publisher warm-up: Original open Estimation Exploration shows one dot on a300–400 interval; offer too-low, about-right and too-high guesses, then revise after educator adds tickmarks. No single publisher correct numerical answer is supplied. 2. Examine source student-facing tasks and materials: Activity1 original compare371 with317 by Diego base-ten picture, Clare digit reasoning, Jada authentic300..400 line with actual dots317 and371. Compare own true dots483 and443 on original400..500 number line;483>443. Activity2 multiple comparisons in original lesson using number lines; genuine student drawings need educator. 3. Show original numeric and place-value strategies: Compare three-digit numbers by hundreds first, then tens, then ones. Equal hundreds371 vs317 differ in tens (7>1), so371>317. On equally spaced0-anchored number line a larger number lies farther right, even if visible diagram is cropped between300 and400. Original unknown estimation remains OPEN; no fabricated exact dot. 4. Discuss plausible misconception and accurate solution with specific source values: False equivalence of greater numerical endpoint with a shorter arbitrary drawing. Treating Jada's original open estimate as exactly350 without source evidence. Don't grade subjective representation preference as one forced choice. 5. Finish actual source images/cards/games/drawings with educator; do NOT invent values not available as text. 6. Complete separate original Edulytics numeric practice with exact re-verification and teacher-graded open parts. 7. Preserve teacher assessment boundary: Educator inspects actual original three student representations and drawn dots, open estimate reasoning and partner comparisons; private Cool-down not copied.",
   "Mathematically inappropriate actions: False equivalence of greater numerical endpoint with a shorter arbitrary drawing. Treating Jada's original open estimate as exactly350 without source evidence. Don't grade subjective representation preference as one forced choice. Do not claim numeric answer certifies original image, physical block/card game, number-line dot or class oral practice.",
   "Source IM2021 G2 Unit5 L9. Compare three-digit numbers by hundreds first, then tens, then ones. Equal hundreds371 vs317 differ in tens (7>1), so371>317. On equally spaced0-anchored number line a larger number lies farther right, even if visible diagram is cropped between300 and400. Original unknown estimation remains OPEN; no fabricated exact dot. Educator inspects actual original three student representations and drawn dots, open estimate reasoning and partner comparisons; private Cool-down not copied."]),
  new(77,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U05:L10","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-5/lesson-10/preparation.html","166e9f20aa6a54030497e7b3d522889aaa575857ff11fb98aaf7403f520c3ac8",
  ["CCSS:2.NBT.A.4","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8"],
  ["Learning goal: You will compare three-digit numbers using your understanding of place value.\nIn grade 1, you compared two-digit numbers using the \\(<\\) , \\(>\\) , and \\(=\\) symbols. In previous lessons, you represented three-digit numbers in different ways and identified the place value of digits in three-digit numbers. In this lesson, you transition from representing and comparing three-digit numbers based on the counting sequence and your location on the number line to focus on reasoning based on place value.",
   "Key ideas:\n- Compare three-digit numbers by reasoning about the value of the digits\n- In grade 1, you compared two-digit numbers using the \\(<\\) , \\(>\\) , and \\(=\\) symbols\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.",
   "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(36 + 40\\) \\(46 + 30\\) \\(59 + 40\\) \\(69 + 30\\)\nApproach: “How could the first expression help you find the value of the second one?” (46 is 10 more than 36, and 30 is 10 less than 40, so the answer is the same. You have the same number of tens.)\nExample 2: Who has more? How do you know? Mai Caption: Mai Tyler Caption: Tyler Mai Tyler Compare the base-ten diagrams. Write each value as a three-digit number. Use the symbols \\(<\\) , \\(>\\) , or \\(=\\) to compare the numbers. \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\phantom{3}\\boxed{\\phantom{33}}\\phantom{3}\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\)...\nApproach: “Which value is greater? How do you know?” (The two quantities are equal. Both diagrams represent 224. One had 2 hundreds, but the other had 1 hundred and 12 tens.) Record \\(224 = 224\\) . “When comparing numbers based on base-ten diagrams, what do you think about to help you decide which quantity is greater? Explain.” (First we need to find out how many hundreds each number has, but sometimes that means we have...",
   "Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.",
   "Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.",
   "You will compare three-digit numbers using your understanding of place value. In grade 1, you compared two-digit numbers using the \\(<\\) , \\(>\\) , and \\(=\\) symbols."],
