  new(78,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U05:L11","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-5/lesson-11/preparation.html","45981e0a7d67058f758483b1f4ec7a37860a3c851227af05aac796a66f878be2",
  ["CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.3","CCSS:2.NBT.A.4"],
  ["Learning goal: You will compare three-digit numbers using your understanding of place value.\nIn previous lessons, you learned to compare three-digit numbers using the number line and base-ten diagrams. In this lesson, you compare three-digit numbers in tasks that do not suggest a particular representation. In the first activity, you complete comparison statements to make them true and are encouraged to explain or show your thinking in a way that makes sense to them.",
   "Key ideas:\n- Compare three-digit numbers using place value understanding\n- In previous lessons, you learned to compare three-digit numbers using the number line and base-ten diagrams\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.",
   "Example 1: Decide if each statement is true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. \\(86 > 80 + 4\\) \\(400 + 40 + 6 < 846\\) \\(330 < 300 + 3\\) \\(500 + 50 > 505\\)\nApproach: “How could expanded form help you decide whether the expression \\(330 < 300 + 3\\) is true or false?” (I knew that 30 is 3 tens and 3 is only 3 ones, so 330 is greater than 303.)\nExample 2: Compare the numbers. \\(>\\) , \\(=\\) , or \\(<\\) 521 \\(\\boxed{\\phantom{33}}\\) 523 Explain or show your thinking. If it helps, use a diagram or number line. \\(>\\) , \\(=\\) , or \\(<\\) 889 \\(\\boxed{\\phantom{33}}\\) 878 Explain or show your thinking. If it helps, use a diagram or number line. Place the numbers in the blanks to make each comparison true. Use each...\nApproach: “We decided that this was a true statement. How does the expanded form of these numbers help justify our thinking?” (We can see the value of each place, so we can compare each digit.)",
   "Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.",
   "Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.",
